# THE VERSE-DEV DRIVE: the charter staging runs.
#
# Two halves, six strata and twelve rules, in one directory because they are one program: the
# REVIEW (this file) reads what has landed since it last looked, against the tree as it stands
# now, and remembers what it found; and the MERGE GATE (merge.tony) turns what the review's fixes
# deliver into a proposal somebody decides. Install it whole —
#
#     verse charter -a -d .verse/charters/comment-drift
#
# — which installs both as one step, and within each the strata first whatever order they
# appear in, so the ordering is in force before a rule names it.
#
# The two halves keep their own numbering — the review's rules are 1..5, the gate's are 1..3
# with a 1b and 1c — because the prose in each refers to its own by number.
#
# comment-drift-states.md beside the directory reads the whole thing as state machines — which field each
# rule writes, and which arrows nothing draws — for a reader who wants the shape before the
# reasons.
#
# ── THE UNIT OF REVIEW IS A RANGE, and that is what changed ──────────────────────
#
# It reviewed main's TIP: one commit per push, whatever else the push carried. A push of three
# reviewed one of them, and a comment that started lying in the middle commit was never looked
# at. The obvious repair — review every commit — is worse, and the drive found out why before it
# was built: it reviews ancient commits whose tree no longer exists, files findings about symbols
# that have since moved, and proposes fixes against a checkout forty commits stale. The commit is
# the OCCASION to look; it is not the subject matter.
#
# So the unit is `last-reviewed..head`, reviewed against the tree at the far end. What that
# dissolves, all at once:
#
#   - no ancient commits: the range starts where the last review got to, and what it judges is
#     current code;
#   - no storm on first install: seed the cursor at the tip and the first range is empty;
#   - no linearity to reason about: a range is a TREE DIFF, so merges, fan-out at a fork and
#     non-fast-forward history are git's problem rather than the charter's;
#   - retry is free: a review that fails leaves the cursor where it was, so the next one covers
#     the same range plus whatever landed since. Delay makes the range bigger, not the backlog
#     longer.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, stated rather than discovered: per-commit attribution. A comment broken in one
# commit of a push and fixed in the next nets out and is never filed — which is correct, since
# the tree at the far end is what a comment has to be true about, but it is a behaviour change
# from reviewing each commit in turn.
#
# ── SEEDING, which is two writes and not one ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# The cursor needs somewhere to stand, and where it stands is what the first range starts from:
#
#     verse data repos.verse.drift.at --put '{sha: <main's tip>, stage: idle}'
#
# Nothing fires until main moves, so a first install has no backlog whatever the source's window
# holds — which is the storm answered by construction rather than by a knob.
#
# ON A VERSE THAT RAN THE OLDER CHARTER there is one more step, and skipping it loses the ledger:
# findings written before this were addressed under (rule, subject, key) and the ones written
# after are addressed by symbol, so the old rows can never be closed by a verdict and would be
# gathered forever. Copy each open one to its symbol and remove the original:
#
#     verse data repos.verse.drift.finding.<symbol> --put '<its payload>'
#     verse data repos.verse.drift.finding.<old composed id> --rm

# ── THE REVIEW ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# THERE IS NO `reflected` STRATUM HERE ANY MORE, and its absence is the shape of the change: no
# rule in this half has a git entity as its SUBJECT. The tip is read — by a condition, which is
# what wakes these rules when it moves — and everything acted on belongs to this package. The
# gate half (merge.tony) still owns one, because a delivered branch is a git ref and that is its subject.
at: repos.verse.drift
reads: [git]
strata:
# The cursor is one entity: where the review has got to. It BOUNCES — idle → claimed → idle —
# and a PATCH is what moves it, which is the one shape that can carry a moving value: a derive's
# address is a function of (rule, subject) and its content may reference identity and never
# state, precisely so that re-deriving writes the same bytes twice. A single slot everybody
# overwrites is what those two rules exist to forbid, so the cursor cannot be derived by anybody
# — it is patched, by rules whose subject it is (docs/examples/level-ping-pong.yaml: recursion
# inside one level is what the ordering permits).
- name: cursor
  level: 10
  match:
    path: "repos.verse.drift.at"

# One entity per range, keyed by the commit it covers — its FAR end, and not the near one. The
# chain is serial, so `to` determines `from` and an id carrying both would be a separator
# convention to parse; and it is `to` that every reader downstream names, since `train-load` asks
# for the review of the tip main is standing on (`drift.range.<tip>.report`). `from` rides in the
# payload for whoever wants the span.
#
# It is also what gives each review its own TASK ID: a task's id is (rule, subject), so with the cursor as the subject every review would be
# `review-range@repos.verse.drift.at` — and a spawn on an id that already completed comes back
# instantly carrying the OLD answer, so the range is never looked at and the cursor advances past
# it (git issue 6j911yhzh12kswgjgsn0, found by watching exactly that happen).
- name: range
  level: 20
  match:
    path: "repos.verse.drift.range"

- name: ledger
  level: 30
  match:
    path: "repos.verse.drift.finding"
rules:
# 1. THE CLAIM, and it is the whole race.
#
# The tip is read HERE, when the review is decided, and written down as `covering`. A commit
# landing while the review runs is then neither covered by it nor skipped: rule 5 advances to
# what was CLAIMED rather than to whatever the head says at completion, so the next range starts
# exactly where this one stopped.
#
# `sha: !not .[tip]` is condread.go's own example inverted — fire while the cursor is not
# standing on the tip — and the read joins this trigger's reads, so main moving is what wakes it.
# No `on:` clause: an `on` is a formula over the whole verse, and "a git head exists" rises once
# and then stands, which is a level rather than an edge and never fires again (trigger/edge.go).
- name: claim
  stratum: cursor
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repos.verse.drift.at"
      value: !let
        let:
        - tip: !get-path
            at: "git.verse.head"
            path: sha
        in:
          stage: idle
          sha: !not .[tip]
  action:
    patch:
      stage: claimed
      covering: !get-path
        at: "git.verse.head"
        path: sha
# 2. …and the range it claimed becomes an entity, so the review has a subject of its own.
- name: open-range
  stratum: cursor
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repos.verse.drift.at"
      value:
        stage: claimed
  action:
    derive:
    - path: "repos.verse.drift.range"
      id: $subject.value.covering
      value:
        from: $subject.value.sha
        to: $subject.value.covering
# 3. Before the review, the STANDING SET is read onto the range.
#
# A separate rule rather than a line in the next one, and the reason is ordering: a spawn's
# strings bind against the payload that FIRED the action, so an action that patched `outstanding`
# and then spawned would hand the task the payload from before its own write. Two firings, two
# commits, and the second sees the first. `stage` is the latch that sequences them.
#
# IT READS THE LEDGER, and that only works because rule 4 addresses a finding by its SYMBOL. A
# keyed fan-out lands under (rule, subject, key), and the subject is the review — so a later
# review's `resolved` used to land BESIDE the open row rather than on it, gathering `status:
# open` handed every stale claim back forever, and nothing a review said could ever close one.
# `id:` inside `!each` (c1231f7d) is what makes the address the place plus the symbol, so a
# verdict OVERWRITES the claim it is about.
#
# That is worth more than carrying the standing set from review to review, which was the other
# way to make closure work: carried forward, an answer REPLACES the set, so a reviewer going
# quiet about a finding deletes it. Here silence leaves it open — resolution stays a fact
# somebody wrote rather than an absence inferred from a model saying nothing.
#
# `status: open` is also the human override: set a finding's status to anything else and it stops
# being handed to the reviewer, with no charter change and nothing to argue with.
- name: gather
  stratum: range
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repos.verse.drift.range.*"
      value: !not.has-path stage
  action:
    patch:
      stage: gathered
      outstanding: !list-paths
        at: "repos.verse.drift.finding.*"
        value:
          status: open
# 4. …and the range is reviewed, once, against what is already known.
#
# The latch is `stage: gathered`, which rule 3 writes and all three terminal hooks write past. A
# hook that did not would leave that ending unlatched, and the next thing to touch this entity
# would spawn again.
- name: review-range
  stratum: range
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repos.verse.drift.range.*"
      value:
        stage: gathered
  action:
    spawn:
      # The repository, and what this task may push to it: ONLY a proposal branch of its own,
      # `verse/auto/<task>`, so the deliverable is a diff rather than prose about a diff, and
      # `main` is deliberately refused. A fixing task's work is a PROPOSAL — that is the whole
      # shape of the deliverable — and somewhere with a consequence is merge.tony's spawn, which
      # exists so that reaching it is a different charter rather than a rule somebody remembers.
      # The agent commits and pushes itself; a run that changed nothing pushes nothing and leaves
      # no branch. "Its own" is two rows, on facts the runner fills: a branch not at the forge
      # (`exists`), and one whose tip this task made (`own`). Another task's `verse/auto/*` is
      # neither, so a fast-forward onto it is refused with the rest.
      autonomy:
      - {match: {system: git, verb: push, ref: "refs/heads/verse/auto/*", exists: "false", force: "false", delete: "false"}, then: allow}
      - {match: {system: git, verb: push, ref: "refs/heads/verse/auto/*", own: "true", force: "false", delete: "false"}, then: allow}
      - {match: {system: git, verb: push}, then: deny}
      - {match: {system: git, verb: fetch}, then: allow}
      - {match: {system: files}, then: allow}
      - {match: {system: commands}, then: allow}
      - {match: {system: agent}, then: allow}
      - {match: {system: question}, then: allow}
      - {then: allow}
      agent: {name: verse}
      environment:
        systems:
          network: {mode: open}
          git:
          - repo: signadot/verse
            from: main
            fetch: {}
            push:
            - {ref: "refs/heads/verse/auto/*", exists: "false", force: "false", delete: "false"}
            - {ref: "refs/heads/verse/auto/*", own: "true", force: "false", delete: "false"}
          files: {}
          commands: {}
          agent: {}
          question: {}
      # THE ANSWER IS A CONTRACT rather than a request in the prose. "Your final message is JSON"
      # was obeyed on one run and ignored on the next, and an unparseable answer is not a bad
      # answer — it is no answer: `$data` resolves to nothing, the completion hook is refused,
      # and the review is lost with the record left in no state at all, silently (git issue
      # 91jktrr3h12ksa8fgsn0).
      answer:
        files: !irtype 0
        findings: !irtype []
      prompt: |
        The verse repository is checked out at ./verse with its full history, at $to.

        Review the RANGE $from..$to — everything that has landed since the last review — and
        answer with `at: $to`. The range is what you review; $to is the commit the tree stands
        at, and it is what your answer is a claim about. main can move while you work, so a
        reader must not have to assume which commit you meant.

        WHAT STILL STANDS. These are the findings the last review left open — claims that some
        Go symbol's comments disagree with its code:

        $outstanding

        A finding you do not mention stays open on its own and will be handed to you again, so
        leaving it out is correct and re-listing it unchanged costs tokens for no fact. What you
        answer with is what CHANGED: `status: resolved` for one that is right in the tree you are
        reading, and whatever this range broke.

        Check each carried finding against the TREE, not against your memory of the diff: a
        comment can be fixed by a commit that never touched the file it names, and the range is
        only the reason to look.

        WHAT CHANGED. The Go files the range touched:

            git -C verse diff --name-only $from..$to -- '*.go'

        For each of those files, read what changed in it:

            git -C verse diff $from..$to -- <path>

        and judge the comments on the declarations that changed, plus any comment the change
        made wrong elsewhere in the same file. A comment is STALE when it DISAGREES with the
        code it is attached to: a parameter that is gone, a rationale for a branch that was
        deleted, a "returns X" that returns Y, an algorithm described that was replaced under
        it. Terse, unfashionable and MISSING are not stale — staleness is a disagreement, not a
        gap.

        A range is not a commit, and that changes one thing: a comment broken by one commit and
        corrected by a later one in the SAME range is not a finding. What you are judging is the
        tree at $to, and the intermediate states are somebody else's history.

        FIX each stale one in place with write_file: rewrite the comment to say what the code now
        does, in the voice of the comments around it. Change nothing else — not the code, not the
        formatting, not a comment that is merely unlovely. Run no git command that writes: the
        working tree is the handoff, and delivery is the runner's.

        WHAT YOU DO NOT DO, because the working tree IS the deliverable and every file you leave
        behind is committed: do not write a test, do not run one, do not build, and do not add a
        file. You are judging whether a comment disagrees with the code beside it — never whether
        the code is right. A test you write to satisfy yourself lands in somebody's branch as
        though a reviewer had asked for it.

        A FIX YOU JUST WROTE RESOLVES NOTHING. Your edits are in a working tree that becomes a
        branch, and a branch is a PROPOSAL — somebody has to approve it, and it can be declined,
        or superseded, or retired unmerged while main moves on. So a symbol you fixed is still
        `status: open`, because it is still wrong in the tree you reviewed, and you say where you
        proposed the fix: `proposedAt: $to`. The review that comes after the fix LANDS reads
        main, finds the comment accurate, and resolves it. That is what makes the ledger a record
        of MAIN rather than of what agents believe they did.

        This is not hypothetical bookkeeping. A review fixed `task.Fatals`, marked it resolved,
        and its branch was retired before the merge; main kept the wrong comment while the ledger
        said somebody had dealt with it, and nothing re-examined it, because a resolved finding
        is not handed to anybody again.

        A finding you are handed that already carries `proposedAt` and is STILL WRONG means that
        fix did not land. Fix it again and say `proposedAt: $to` for this attempt: the work is
        cheap and idempotent, where a comment nobody corrects is permanent.

        READ EACH THING ONCE. Re-reading a file you have read does not make a verdict truer, and
        the second read costs a turn the first one already bought. When every changed declaration
        and every carried finding has a verdict, answer.

        Name each symbol the way Go does, repo-relative: `entity.nodeToPayload`,
        `io/github.Reflect`, `entity.(*LogdStore).Get`. That identity survives the comment
        being rewritten and the declaration moving between files, which a line number does not.
        `go doc ./<pkg> <Name>` says whether you have it right.

        YOUR FINAL MESSAGE IS THE ANSWER — a JSON document, nothing before it and nothing after
        it. Every key and every string is quoted, which is the whole of what has gone wrong
        before. Report an entry ONLY for a symbol that has something to say: a symbol you judged
        and found ACCURATE is not an entry, and an empty `findings` list is a real answer.

        EVERY FINDING CARRIES ALL FIVE KEYS — symbol, status, says, at, proposedAt — and
        `proposedAt` is `null` when you proposed no fix, which is every `resolved` one. Write the
        key with a null rather than leaving it out. An absent reference is an error where a null
        one is a value, so a finding missing the key fails the whole filing: not that finding —
        the WHOLE list, including the ones that were complete, and the review is lost with the
        range marked reviewed and the cursor advanced past it. That is not hypothetical; it is
        what the first run of this charter did (18:09Z 2026-09-10, `$item.proposedAt is not
        present in the payload that fired this action`).

            {
              "files": 2,
              "findings": [
                {"symbol": "entity.nodeToPayload",
                 "status": "open",
                 "says": "the comment says it returns nil for an empty map; it returns an empty payload",
                 "at": "$to",
                 "proposedAt": "$to"},
                {"symbol": "trigger.predicateNode",
                 "status": "resolved",
                 "says": "named a function deleted in this range; the comment is accurate now",
                 "at": "$to",
                 "proposedAt": null}
              ]
            }
    onDone:
      derive:
      - path: "repos.verse.drift.finding"
        value: !each
          over: $data.findings
          id: $item.symbol
          value:
            symbol: $item.symbol
            status: $item.status
            says: $item.says
            at: $item.at
            proposedAt: $item.proposedAt
      patch:
        stage: reviewed
        status: done
        report: $data
        err: !delete null
    onFailed:
      patch:
        stage: failed
        status: failed
        err: $err
    onCancelled:
      patch:
        stage: !delete null
        err: !delete null
# 5. …and the cursor steps, but only onto the range it claimed and only once that range is done.
#
# `covering: !or .[done]` is the subject's own field tested against a bound set. The range's
# address cannot be read from a condition — a condition read may not name $subject, since the
# subject is what it selects — so this asks whether what we claimed is among the shas that have
# been reviewed. A failed review leaves `status: failed`, so it is not in that set: the chain
# stops where it broke, visibly, and `verse data repos.verse.drift.at --put '{stage: idle}'` is the
# retry, which by then covers everything since.
- name: advance
  stratum: cursor
  condition:
    subject:
      path: "repos.verse.drift.at"
      value: !let
        let:
        - done: !list-paths
            at: "repos.verse.drift.range.*"
            value:
              status: done
            path: to
        in:
          stage: claimed
          covering: !or .[done]
  action:
    patch:
      sha: $subject.value.covering
      covering: !delete null
      stage: idle
